1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,510
You've read this English sentence up to one hidden character.

2
00:00:03,890 --> 00:00:08,350
With yes-or-no questions, how many do you need per character, on average?

3
00:00:10,200 --> 00:00:11,950
The natural answer is five.

4
00:00:12,330 --> 00:00:17,950
Each answer can cut what's left in half, and five cuts take twenty-seven down to one.

5
00:00:18,400 --> 00:00:25,890
With perfect fifty-fifty splits, the ideal is log base two of twenty-seven, about four point seven five.

6
00:00:26,233 --> 00:00:31,253
In 1951, Claude Shannon estimated the true figure at about one.

7
00:00:31,633 --> 00:00:39,533
Whatever it is, no text compressor can beat it on average, and guessing what comes next is how language models are trained.

8
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:42,700
So where do four of every five questions go?

9
00:00:43,080 --> 00:00:47,240
Splitting the alphabet evenly charges every character the same price.

10
00:00:47,620 --> 00:00:54,160
Z costs as much as E, yet in real text E turns up a hundred and thirty times as often.

11
00:00:54,600 --> 00:00:59,190
Instead, ask questions that split the probability, not the alphabet.

12
00:00:59,570 --> 00:01:02,100
Is it a space, E, T, A, or O?

13
00:01:02,480 --> 00:01:05,160
Those five make up nearly half of all text.

14
00:01:05,500 --> 00:01:07,490
E starts near a tenth of the bar.

15
00:01:07,870 --> 00:01:13,460
Each ideal answer keeps half the probability, so E doubles its share of what's left.

16
00:01:13,840 --> 00:01:17,120
Three point three doublings, and E fills the whole bar.

17
00:01:17,466 --> 00:01:22,076
Z starts at one in twelve hundred and sixty, and needs ten point three.

18
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:29,650
In general, a character with probability p needs k questions, where p times two to the k equals one.

19
00:01:31,150 --> 00:01:37,280
So two to the k equals one over p, and k equals log base two of one over p.

20
00:01:37,633 --> 00:01:40,503
Now give each character its own cost as a height.

21
00:01:40,883 --> 00:01:44,863
Common characters sit wide and low, rare ones narrow and tall.

22
00:01:45,200 --> 00:01:51,190
The widths add up to one, so the total area is the average number of questions per character.

23
00:01:51,570 --> 00:01:55,240
Then pour the skyline flat, and its height is that average.

24
00:01:55,600 --> 00:02:01,520
Shannon called that average the entropy, counted in bits, one bit per yes-or-no answer.

25
00:02:01,900 --> 00:02:04,570
For English letters, it's four point one one.

26
00:02:04,933 --> 00:02:14,023
That beats four point seven five, but it's still four times Shannon's estimate: it prices every character as if nothing came before it.

27
00:02:14,366 --> 00:02:15,866
What comes after a Q?

28
00:02:17,533 --> 00:02:20,263
It's U, ninety-eight times out of a hundred.

29
00:02:20,643 --> 00:02:27,743
U swallows the bar, its cost drops to almost nothing, and the average falls to point one six bits.

30
00:02:28,533 --> 00:02:34,393
After TH, E takes two thirds of the bar, and its cost shrinks to about half a question.

31
00:02:34,733 --> 00:02:42,293
Half a question sounds impossible, but a compressor asks about long runs of letters, and the fractions add up.

32
00:02:42,673 --> 00:02:44,653
Here they average one point seven.

33
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:47,340
Q and TH are the easy cases.

34
00:02:47,720 --> 00:02:52,060
Averaged over every two-letter context, it's still two point eight.

35
00:02:52,500 --> 00:02:59,990
Shannon measured this with a person, who read a passage one character at a time, guessing each next one until right.

36
00:03:00,370 --> 00:03:03,890
Seventy-nine of a hundred and two came on the first guess.

37
00:03:04,233 --> 00:03:09,473
A twin who guesses the same way could rebuild the passage from these counts alone.

38
00:03:09,853 --> 00:03:16,203
Treat each guess count as a symbol, and the same skyline pours flat at about one point four bits.

39
00:03:16,583 --> 00:03:18,683
A better guesser could only lower it.

40
00:03:19,033 --> 00:03:26,693
Shannon's tests, with a hundred letters of context, put English between point six and one point three bits per character.

41
00:03:27,033 --> 00:03:37,473
In twenty twenty-three, a language model compressing part of a new book in the same twenty-seven symbols needed point eight four bits, inside that range.

42
00:03:37,933 --> 00:03:42,323
The hidden character from the start is the Y at the end of the other day.

43
00:03:42,703 --> 00:03:47,293
Shannon's subject got it on the first guess, a single yes-or-no question.

44
00:03:47,673 --> 00:03:49,603
Splitting the alphabet took five.

45
00:03:49,983 --> 00:03:53,453
In English, almost no letter arrives without a past.

